            "is_markdown": true,
            "text": "One thing that jumps out at me here is that it suggests is.na(int) is now faster than anyNA(int) if I'm reading this correctly, right? like you could implement a faster anyNA(int) by do is.na(int) and then looping over the resulting vector (Which won't be an altrep) and return true if/when you find one?. That doesn't seem right, but I haven't had a chance to apply the patch and look into it further.",
            "bug_id": 19141
          },
          {
            "tags": [],
            "is_private": false,
            "id": 103579,
            "creation_time": "2026-09-03T18:33:26Z",
            "count": 2,
            "text": "Your reading of the table is right, and it reproduces -- it is not an\nartifact. On the patched build, one full scan by is.na() over 10^7\nintegers takes 0.85 ms while anyNA() takes 2.6 ms, even though anyNA()\nallocates nothing and is.na() writes a 40 MB answer.\n\nThe reason is not ALTREP dispatch: anyNA() already reads by region, so\nit pays no per-element dispatch either. The difference is the shape of\nthe inner loop. anyNA()'s body is\n\n    for (int k = 0; k < nbatch; k++)\n        if (xI[k] == NA_INTEGER)\n            return true;\n\nThe early exit is a data-dependent branch, so the compiler cannot\nvectorise the loop; it runs an element at a time. The patched is.na()\nloop is branchless --\n\n    pa[i + k] = (xI[k] == NA_INTEGER);\n\n-- so it vectorises and runs at memory bandwidth, which more than pays\nfor the allocation and the writes. I confirmed it is the loop shape\nand nothing else: a .Call() replica of anyNA()'s exact loop over\nINTEGER(x) also takes 2.3 ms.\n\nSo yes: in the no-NA case, is.na()-then-scan would beat today's\nanyNA() at the C level. But it allocates n*4 bytes and gives up the\nearly exit, which is anyNA()'s whole advantage the moment NAs are\nactually present -- the anyNA() row in the table is its worst case, a\nvector with no NAs. (At the R level the composite loses outright:\nany(is.na(x)) measures 6.2 ms here, since any() also reads its\nargument an element at a time.)\n\nThe clean resolution is inside anyNA(): accumulate the NA test\nbranchlessly over a fixed-size chunk and branch only between chunks.\nThe inner loop vectorises again, and the early exit survives at chunk\ngranularity -- at 1024 elements a bounded cost well under a\nmicrosecond. Times in ms, same machine and protocol as the table\nabove (10^7 elements, no NAs unless said otherwise):\n\n                                    before    after\n    anyNA(<integer>)                  2.60     0.50\n    anyNA(<integer>), NA at 100       0.00     0.00\n    anyNA(<integer>), NA at 10^7      2.60     0.50\n    anyNA(<double>)                   2.55     1.00\n    anyNA(<logical>)                  3.35     0.55\n    is.na(<integer>), for contrast    0.85     0.85\n\nanyNA() is again at least as fast as is.na() everywhere. The double\ncase gains less because it reads twice the bytes; it is memory-bound\nafter the change.\n\nThe diff below also does two adjacent things it seemed wrong to leave:\nthe LGLSXP case read LOGICAL_ELT() an element at a time (the same\nshape the main patch removes elsewhere -- it is the 6x line above),\nand the induction variable becomes R_xlen_t: on the DATAPTR path\nnbatch is the full vector length, so `int k` overflows for a\nmaterialised long vector, though you need more than 2^31 elements to\nhit it. CPLXSXP and STRSXP are left alone (complex is memory-bound at\n16 bytes an element, and there is no STRING_GET_REGION).\n\nThe diff applies on top of attachment 3796, and equally to plain trunk\n(the main patch does not touch anyNA(); verified both). Checked with\nNAs placed at chunk boundaries (positions 1, 1023, 1024, 1025, 2048,\n4097, n), empty vectors, NaN vs NA for doubles, logical NA, and\nrecursive lists.\n\n```\nIndex: src/main/coerce.c\n===================================================================\n--- src/main/coerce.c (trunk + attachment 3796)\n+++ src/main/coerce.c (working copy)\n@@ -2370,15 +2370,31 @@ static bool anyNA(SEXP call, SEXP op, SEXP args, SEXP env)\n     }\n\n     R_xlen_t i, n = xlength(x);\n+    /* Scan in chunks, accumulating the NA test branchlessly within a\n+       chunk and only branching between chunks: a per-element early\n+       exit is a data-dependent branch the compiler cannot vectorise\n+       past.  ALTREP regions are at most 512 elements, so there the\n+       chunk loop collapses to one test per region. */\n+    const R_xlen_t chunksize = 1024;\n     switch (xT) {\n     case REALSXP:\n     {\n      if(REAL_NO_NA(x))\n          return false;\n      ITERATE_BY_REGION(x, xD, i, nbatch, double, REAL, {\n-             for (int k = 0; k < nbatch; k++)\n-                 if (ISNAN(xD[k]))\n+             R_xlen_t k = 0;\n+             while (k < nbatch) {\n+                 R_xlen_t kend = k + chunksize;\n+                 if (kend > nbatch)\n+                     kend = nbatch;\n+\n+                 int found = 0;\n+                 for (; k < kend; k++)\n+                     found |= ISNAN(xD[k]);\n+\n+                 if (found)\n                      return true;\n+             }\n          });\n      break;\n     }\n@@ -2387,16 +2403,39 @@ static bool anyNA(SEXP call, SEXP op, SEXP args, SEXP env)\n      if(INTEGER_NO_NA(x))\n          return false;\n      ITERATE_BY_REGION(x, xI, i, nbatch, int, INTEGER, {\n-             for (int k = 0; k < nbatch; k++)\n-                 if (xI[k] == NA_INTEGER)\n+             R_xlen_t k = 0;\n+             while (k < nbatch) {\n+                 R_xlen_t kend = k + chunksize;\n+                 if (kend > nbatch)\n+                     kend = nbatch;\n+\n+                 int found = 0;\n+                 for (; k < kend; k++)\n+                     found |= (xI[k] == NA_INTEGER);\n+\n+                 if (found)\n                      return true;\n+             }\n          });\n      break;\n     }\n     case LGLSXP:\n     {\n-     for (i = 0; i < n; i++)\n-         if (LOGICAL_ELT(x, i) == NA_LOGICAL) return true;\n+     ITERATE_BY_REGION(x, xL, i, nbatch, int, LOGICAL, {\n+             R_xlen_t k = 0;\n+             while (k < nbatch) {\n+                 R_xlen_t kend = k + chunksize;\n+                 if (kend > nbatch)\n+                     kend = nbatch;\n+\n+                 int found = 0;\n+                 for (; k < kend; k++)\n+                     found |= (xL[k] == NA_LOGICAL);\n+\n+                 if (found)\n+                     return true;\n+             }\n+         });\n      break;\n     }\n     case CPLXSXP:\n```",
